This is still not efficient enough. Here's a solution that leverages the fact that the maximum beautiful set can be constructed by taking all points where one coordinate is even and the other is odd, and then selecting a subset where no two points have integer distance.

The key insight is that the maximum beautiful set is the larger of the two sets: all points with x even and y odd, or all points with x odd and y even. This is because any two points in the same parity group (both even or both odd) will have integer distance, but points from different parity groups will not.

